    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you provide a comparative analysis between signalized intersections and modern roundabout designs in terms of vehicle throughput and delay mitigation? I'm specifically looking at how these two configurations handle peak-hour congestion and the concept of \"flow friction\" in high-density urban environments.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Modern roundabouts generally outperform signalized intersections in handling peak-hour congestion due to their continuous flow design. Roundabouts eliminate the full stop-start cycles common at signals, significantly reducing vehicular delay and \"flow friction.\" While signals must manage conflicting movements discretely, roundabouts manage them dynamically through yield priority, encouraging smoother, more consistent traffic flow even under high density. Ultimately, the loss of signal-related stopping time makes roundabouts more efficient for uninterrupted urban movement.",
      "source": "target",
