    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you expand a bit more on that \"flow friction\" part? I'm particularly interested in the unpredictable elements—those sudden drops in efficiency or the irregular disruptions that don't seem to follow the standard mathematical models of capacity. What causes those specific types of systemic interruptions in a roundabout setting?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "\"Flow friction\" in this context refers to localized inefficiencies caused by operational disruptions that aren't simple saturation flow issues. In roundabouts, unpredictable drops often arise from driver hesitation, inadequate lane discipline (e.g., sudden cutting in), or overly aggressive merging/yielding behavior. These human behavioral factors create momentary jams or queue spillback because the system relies heavily on predictable yielding behavior. Unlike the structured interference of a signal, these systemic interruptions are inherently stochastic and reliant on driver compliance.",
      "source": "target",
