    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Right, so it's mostly human error and driver psychology then. But are there any architectural or external factors that contribute to that \"stochastic\" feeling? Like, are there specific geometric flaws or environmental variables that seem to invite more of that chaotic, disruptive energy into the flow, making it feel less like a controlled system and more like just random interference?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, geometric flaws significantly amplify stochastic disruptions in roundabouts. Insufficient entry lane length or inadequate splitter island design can force erratic merging maneuvers, leading to driver uncertainty and hesitation. Furthermore, poor sightlines due to adjacent buildings or excessive street furniture can obscure weaving paths, inviting risky behaviors. Environmental factors like sudden pedestrian surges or unpredictable weather can also exacerbate these minor geometric weaknesses, introducing sudden, chaotic variables into the generally smooth operation.",
      "source": "target",
